/-!
# Starting mid-sized groups over F₂ and eliminating U₃(3)

The source target is Theorem 13.3.16: under Hypothesis 13.3.1, the faithful
action quotient `L/C_L(V)` is not isomorphic to `U₃(3)`. The proof continues
through Lemmas 13.3.19–20 on printed p.896.

This module exports the original-module weak-closure construction and the
unitary source exclusion conditional on the preceding Chapter12/13 theorems.
The maximal-escape action, nondegenerate-plane centralizer bound and unitary
13.3.14 noncentrality give the strict rank bound and an actual escaping prime
actor. The same geometry supplies the rank-three centralizer bound in 4.4.3.
The completed relative-Sylow argument gives uniqueness for its fixed residual.
The actual normal component contains the whole chosen module and residual;
its J₄ and Rudvalis alternatives are now excluded, proving odd-normalizer
control and the original terminal contradiction.

The seven-row module theorem and earlier fixed-space clause now construct
the full natural marking and invariant flag. The exclusion covers both the
literal L/O₂(L) and faithful L/C_L(V) quotients, using their canonical comparison.
It supplies the actual unitary input to the five-row D dispatcher. Earlier
structure and 12.2.13 still have their universal signatures in this application;
the guarded same-G,T adaptation and final earlier-source discharge remain.

The A₅ setup also derives the local parabolic action and original normalizer
controls. If the Baumann normalizer escapes the maximal overgroup, the
original component is an A₅ block, the full and local modules coincide, and
the paired Baumann quotient is marked. In the positive fixed-center case,
the original residual Sylow subgroup supplies an actual transvection on the
selected reduced HStar orbit module. Its normal closure generates the moved
module, on which the literal centralizer quotient acts faithfully and satisfies
every hypothesis of G.6.4. The original paired axes and their actual block
centralizers are also identified. The classified submodule is exactly the
original commutator subgroup [W,H], with its literal ambient inclusion.
The literal wreath identification and the three-free maximal subgroup now
exclude the paired natural-five row, leaving the two binary planes of total
dimension four. Their literal orthogonal recognition preserves the original
action and both plane markings. The zero-center normalizer argument is now
complete, so an escaping normalizer forces this orthogonal action without a
separate fixed-center premise. The containing-component argument now excludes
that escape: the contained layer forces the forbidden A₇ quotient, while the
actual deleted-five block is excluded in every possible simple central
quotient. Both center branches therefore give the original A₅ Baumann
normalizer containment.

The compared-action extension now carries the literal source action through
the minimal-parabolic sieve, reducing the positive fixed-center case to the
paired binary GL₂ row. The zero-center extension identifies the original
Baumann subgroup with a product of four D₈ factors, through its two original
commuting centralizer subgroups. The normalizer action on their canonical
central enlargements and its two invariant elementary choices complete the
zero-center normalizer control.

In the actual multiple-overgroup branch, the minimal original setup now has
its proved full-module cubic bound. The original component-normalizer clause
and A₅ covering-uniqueness theorem are also exported. In the proper-seed
branch, the local cubic bound now supplies full/local equality, moved-quotient
irreducibility and crossed centralization. All-vector Sylow promotion connects
this branch to point control and the weak-closure lower bound. The same-seed
branch supplies the marked tensor model. The original minimal-choice consumer
combines both alternatives and discharges Baumann containment, retaining the
local cubic bound and tensor exclusion. The complete point-component inspection
now removes all carrier and marking inputs, including fixed-kernel points.
The weak-closure endgame
reduces the chosen irreducible case to the natural module with w=n(H)=2,
retaining the earlier support, point-centralizer and Hall-placement inputs.

The reselected minimal A5 setup now gives full normalization and an actual
Chapter12 module choice after tensor exclusion. Its uniform callback uses
the multiple-overgroup hypothesis within the same ambient group; Hall
placement and the natural-generation contradiction are discharged.

Source: Aschbacher–Smith, *The Classification of Quasithin Groups*, combined
manuscript (1 March 2004), §13.3, printed pp.884–896 (PDF pp.901–913).
-/
